# Guard Feedback Authoring

Guard feedback has **two channels with different readers**. Writing one in the other's register
is the recurring defect.

| Channel | Field | Reader | Job |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Advisory** | `additionalContext` | the agent, mid-turn | get the next action right |
| **Deny** | `outcome.deny.reason` | the operator, deciding whether to override | justify the block, name the remedy |

## The advisory shape

Four parts, nothing else:

1. **Guard-id header** — `[guard-name] <what happened, one clause>`.
2. **Quoted evidence** — the matched phrase(s), verbatim, one per line. The highest-value part:
   it tells the agent which text tripped the guard, so it can recognize a false positive instead
   of complying blindly.
3. **Imperative directive** — what to do now, one sentence.
4. **Legitimate-halt branch** — the conditions under which NOT doing it is correct, and what to
   say instead. Without this the guard pressures the agent into acting when stopping was right.

Part 2 invites a CHECKABLE claim, not a costless exit — the agent owes evidence and a kind
(pattern-false / semantic-false) per `claim-confidence.mdc`; quote exactly so it can be checked.

```
[turn-end-untaken-action] You named a next action and did not take it.

  - ill-action: "I'll implement it"

Take it now in this continuation, then report the result. If it genuinely cannot proceed — you
are blocked on a principal decision, a red check, or an external condition you have already
armed a watcher for — name which in one line and end.
```

## The directive has to fit the shape of the fire, not just the guard

Part 3 above says "what to do now" — but a guard that matches more than one SHAPE of input owes
each shape its own directive. A directive that does not fit is worse than a vague one: it invites
the agent to read a TRUE positive as a false one, and a detector whose correct output gets
dismissed has lost the thing it exists for (the mem#719 dynamic, arrived at from the other
direction).

This binds hardest where a guard has taken over a sibling's slot. A dedup between guards on
different events is a **handoff** (mem#831), and a handoff transfers an obligation, not just a
speaking turn: the winner has to carry the loser's REMEDY for the inputs it absorbed. mt#3620 gave
`turn-end-untaken-action` the overlap with `ask-routing-deferral` and kept only its own
commitment-shaped "take it now" — so an offer-shaped closing sentence, whose correct disposition
is usually to retract the sentence, was told to perform the action instead. mt#3767 split the
directive; the fix is one conditional, and the discipline is to notice the obligation at handoff
time rather than a fix later.

**When you add a branch, it becomes the guard's worst case.** Declare a `worstCaseCanary` posed at
it — saturated on EVERY axis at once (the longest branch AND the evidence list at its cap), not
just the axis you changed. Posing it on one axis is how mt#3767's first attempt measured 383 on a
branch whose real worst case was 474, over a 450 ceiling; the same under-posing had already hidden
that the guard's ORIGINAL directive was over the ceiling at cap.

## A redirect is only as available as the tool it names

When your denial text says "use `X` instead", you have made two claims: that `X` does the job, and
that `X` **is reachable at the moment the agent reads this**. The second one is the one nobody
checks.

**State the CAPABILITY boundary — what the named tool cannot do — never an environment boundary.**
Environments move; capabilities are the stable thing. `block-git-gh-cli.ts` said *"For sessions, use
`session_exec(...)`"* long after all four carved-out commands gained a `session` parameter, so the
main-workspace-vs-session split it drew named no real boundary and sent agents to the worse path
(mem#1078 → mt#4226).

**Availability is a capability boundary too, and it is the one that bites hardest.** An `mcp__*`
tool does not degrade when its server is down — it is absent, and cannot be called at all. So ask:

1. **Can the tool I am naming be missing when this guard fires?** Every `mcp__*` name has a server
   behind it. A guard handling a failure mode that ALSO kills that server points at a dead end —
   `block-git-gh-cli.ts` redirects `gh pr view` to `mcp__github__pull_request_read`, which is gone
   in exactly the Docker-down case an agent reaching for `gh` is usually in (mt#3779).
2. **Does my fallback share the failure mode?** This is the easy one to miss. That guard carves out
   `session_exec` so it never "denies the very fallback its own denial text offers" — correct
   reasoning about a fallback the GUARD blocks, and silent about a fallback that does not EXIST.
   `session_exec` is `mcp__minsky__session_exec`: when the Minsky server goes down it takes the
   redirect targets and the escape hatch together, leaving a denial that names ~34 uncallable tools
   and no path at all. Observed twice during mt#4257, where reading `.git/HEAD` by hand was the only
   way left to see git state.
3. **If the answer to either is yes, say so in the denial, and name the override.** An agent with no
   stated path routes around the guard; an agent handed `MINSKY_HOOK_OVERRIDE=<guard>` takes the
   audited one. `block-git-gh-cli.ts`'s `REDIRECT_UNAVAILABLE_ESCAPE` is the worked example.

**A tool name in a `reason` string is a claim with a shelf life.** Nothing re-reads your guard when
the tool surface changes. So when you ship an MCP tool covering a previously-uncovered command, grep
the guards for that command and update their redirect text in the same PR — and when you name a tool
from a server that can be down, write the unavailable case in from the start.

